// What joins a module directory's segments, and it is `/` and not `.` because the join has to be
// invertible: `moduleDirectoryContains` is a segment test, and a segment test on a joined string is
// only sound if no segment can contain the joiner.
//
// ⚠ `.` would be a wrong answer, because a directory name may contain one on every host the compiler
// targets (this repository holds `.maxon/` and `.claude/`). Two silent failures would follow:
//
//   - `feature/` and `feature.extra/` are siblings. Joined with `.` the second reads as
//     `feature.extra`, which starts with `feature.`, so an unmarked declaration in `feature/` would be
//     visible from `feature.extra/`, the widening the separator guard exists to prevent.
//   - `my.dir/` and `my/dir/` would both join to `my.dir`, so two structurally different directories
//     would become one module and each could see the other's unmarked declarations.
//
// `/` cannot appear in a path segment on any host the compiler targets: it is the separator itself on
// Linux, macOS and WASI, and on Windows it is both an accepted separator and one of the characters a
// file or directory name may not contain. So the join is injective and the segment test is sound. The
// `.`-joined spelling the language shows a user is derived from this one
// (`namespaceForModuleDirectory`) and is never what a visibility question is asked on.
export let PortablePathSeparator = "/"
export let WindowsPathSeparator = "\\"

let PortablePathSeparatorByte = 0x2F
let WindowsPathSeparatorByte = 0x5C
